1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,375
Tani Yutaka, the “Tiger of Malaya”:
The Man and the Wartime Legend

2
00:00:05,375 --> 00:00:09,124
On the 17th of March
1942, a month after the

3
00:00:09,204 --> 00:00:12,691
fall of Singapore, a 30-year-old Japanese

4
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:16,600
man died of malaria in
a Singapore hospital.

5
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:21,027
He had grown up in Kuala Terengganu,
spoke Malay, had led a gang

6
00:00:21,107 --> 00:00:25,534
of robbers in the north of the
peninsula, and had been recruited

7
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:30,050
by Japanese military intelligence
the year before the invasion.

8
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:33,175
His name was Tani Yutaka.

9
00:00:33,175 --> 00:00:37,174
Within a year, newspapers in
Japan had made him a hero,

10
00:00:37,254 --> 00:00:42,075
and a feature film called him
Harimau Malai, the Tiger of Malaya.

11
00:00:42,075 --> 00:00:45,587
Much of what is told about
him comes from those wartime

12
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:48,592
accounts and from books
written decades later,

13
00:00:48,672 --> 00:00:52,525
and the few records from his
own time tell a plainer story.

14
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:55,992
According to Japanese
accounts of his life,

15
00:00:56,072 --> 00:01:01,106
Tani Yutaka was born on the 6th
of November 1911 near Fukuoka,

16
00:01:01,186 --> 00:01:04,650
the eldest son of a barber, Tani Urakichi.

17
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:08,724
When he was about two, the family
moved to Kuala Terengganu on

18
00:01:08,804 --> 00:01:13,025
the east coast of Malaya, where
his father opened a barbershop.

19
00:01:13,025 --> 00:01:16,954
His father wanted him
educated in Japan, so he was

20
00:01:17,034 --> 00:01:20,882
sent back to his grandparents
for primary school,

21
00:01:20,962 --> 00:01:24,650
and in 1927, at 15, he
returned to Terengganu.

22
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:29,866
He grew up among Malay friends,
and around 1930, by these accounts,

23
00:01:29,946 --> 00:01:34,925
he became a Muslim and took the
name Mohammad Ali bin Abdullah.

24
00:01:34,925 --> 00:01:38,018
The photograph of him seated in a songkok,

25
00:01:38,098 --> 00:01:41,800
baju and sarong is the
best known picture of him.

26
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,433
In 1931 he went back to Japan

27
00:01:44,513 --> 00:01:47,600
for his conscription examination.

28
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:51,427
He was below the army's minimum
height and was not taken

29
00:01:51,507 --> 00:01:55,263
for active service, and he
stayed on in Fukuoka working

30
00:01:55,343 --> 00:01:59,250
briefly for a footwear company
and then at an ironworks.

31
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:03,200
His father died in Malaya
at the end of that year.

32
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,686
On the morning of the
6th of November 1933,

33
00:02:06,766 --> 00:02:11,825
a killing in Kuala Terengganu
changed the course of his life.

34
00:02:11,825 --> 00:02:15,009
The Malaya Tribune reported
it three days later

35
00:02:15,089 --> 00:02:18,700
under the headline "Little
Girl's Head Chopped Off".

36
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,849
At about 7 o'clock that morning,
it said, a Chinese man "armed

37
00:02:22,929 --> 00:02:27,078
with a large parang" entered the
house of a Japanese man named

38
00:02:27,158 --> 00:02:31,250
Yamada and struck him on the
head, "badly wounding the man".

39
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:34,317
He then went into another
Japanese house and,

40
00:02:34,397 --> 00:02:37,114
finding no one there, into the next one,

41
00:02:37,194 --> 00:02:41,250
"occupied by a Japanese barber
named Tani and his family".

42
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:44,600
There he killed an eight-year-old girl.

43
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:47,984
A crowd had gathered at
the first victim's cries,

44
00:02:48,064 --> 00:02:52,800
and after a chase the man was
"overpowered and taken into custody".

45
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:56,454
The paper reported that he
was believed to be sane,

46
00:02:56,534 --> 00:02:59,975
but that no motive "has
as yet been disclosed".

47
00:02:59,975 --> 00:03:03,575
The crime took place
near the Customs Office.

48
00:03:03,575 --> 00:03:08,682
Japanese accounts name the girl as
Shizuko, Tani's youngest half-sister,

49
00:03:08,762 --> 00:03:13,300
who was in bed with a fever
upstairs when the attacker came in.

50
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:16,886
They describe the attack as
anger over Japan's seizure

51
00:03:16,966 --> 00:03:20,009
of Manchuria two years
earlier, and the common

52
00:03:20,089 --> 00:03:23,200
version holds that the
killer was never caught,

53
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:27,150
though the Malaya Tribune
reported his arrest on the day.

54
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:30,925
Tani himself was in Japan at the time.

55
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:35,683
In June 1934 the rest of the
family left Terengganu for Japan,

56
00:03:35,763 --> 00:03:37,244
and later that year,

57
00:03:37,324 --> 00:03:41,850
according to these accounts,
he went back to Malaya alone.

58
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:45,319
What he did over the next
six years is known almost

59
00:03:45,399 --> 00:03:48,171
entirely from Japanese books and from the

60
00:03:48,251 --> 00:03:51,800
recollections of the men
who later worked with him.

61
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:55,294
By these accounts, he gathered
a band of Malay friends

62
00:03:55,374 --> 00:03:58,670
who robbed mainly Chinese
merchants and moved about

63
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:02,310
the peninsula and southern
Thailand, and he spoke Malay

64
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:05,700
and Thai so well that
people took him for a Malay.

65
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:09,863
He was eventually arrested in
Hat Yai, in southern Thailand,

66
00:04:09,943 --> 00:04:12,479
and was in prison there when Japanese

67
00:04:12,559 --> 00:04:15,600
military intelligence
came looking for him.

68
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:18,611
Japan's army was preparing for a war in

69
00:04:18,691 --> 00:04:22,575
Southeast Asia and wanted
agents who knew Malaya.

70
00:04:22,575 --> 00:04:28,275
According to Japanese accounts, in
early 1941 an agent named Kamoto Toshio,

71
00:04:28,355 --> 00:04:31,049
a former police officer in Manchukuo

72
00:04:31,129 --> 00:04:35,211
working for the army under
cover of a trading company,

73
00:04:35,291 --> 00:04:40,300
paid Tani's bail and persuaded
him to work for the Japanese army.

74
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,400
Tani brought his old gang back together.

75
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,147
The same accounts credit them
with surveying the British

76
00:04:47,227 --> 00:04:50,905
defensive line being built
at Jitra, in northern Kedah,

77
00:04:50,985 --> 00:04:54,675
and with damaging materials
and equipment at the site.

78
00:04:54,675 --> 00:04:59,219
Japanese troops broke through
the Jitra line on the 12th

79
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:03,675
of December 1941, four days
after the invasion began.

80
00:05:03,675 --> 00:05:07,369
During the campaign he
worked with the F Kikan,

81
00:05:07,449 --> 00:05:11,625
the intelligence unit led
by Major Fujiwara Iwaichi.

82
00:05:11,625 --> 00:05:15,765
One of his tasks, according to
these accounts, was to remove the

83
00:05:15,845 --> 00:05:20,513
demolition charges that retreating
British troops had placed on bridges,

84
00:05:20,593 --> 00:05:24,550
and the bridges were often blown
before he could reach them.

85
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:28,100
Somewhere on the way
south he caught malaria.

86
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:32,300
Japanese accounts say he refused
the quinine the army had captured

87
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:37,229
from the British, saying a true Malay
would not take a white man's medicine,

88
00:05:37,309 --> 00:05:41,200
and that he grew so ill he had
to be carried on a stretcher.

89
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:44,100
On the 1st of February he was admitted

90
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:46,925
to a field hospital in Johor Bahru.

91
00:05:46,925 --> 00:05:50,370
After Singapore fell on
the 15th of February,

92
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:54,601
Fujiwara ordered him moved
to a hospital in Singapore,

93
00:05:54,681 --> 00:05:59,225
where he died on the 17th of
March 1942, at the age of 30.

94
00:05:59,225 --> 00:06:02,804
His men are said to have
taken his body and buried it

95
00:06:02,884 --> 00:06:06,750
with Muslim rites, and where
he was buried is not known.

96
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:10,748
Because he had been working
for the army, he was recorded

97
00:06:10,828 --> 00:06:14,325
as having died in the war,
and he was enshrined at

98
00:06:14,405 --> 00:06:18,125
the Yasukuni Shrine in Tokyo
despite being a Muslim.

99
00:06:18,125 --> 00:06:21,388
A memorial stone bearing
his name and dates

100
00:06:21,468 --> 00:06:25,200
stands in the Japanese
Cemetery Park in Hougang.

101
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:29,925
After his death, the Japanese
press at home made him a hero.

102
00:06:29,925 --> 00:06:34,640
Newspapers and the weekly Shukan
Asahi described him as a man

103
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:39,750
who gave money, food and other
necessities to poor local people.

104
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:44,202
In June 1943 the film studio
Daiei released Marai no

105
00:06:44,282 --> 00:06:49,250
Tora (The Tiger of Malaya),
with Nakata Koji in the lead.

106
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:53,283
The film was set in Kota Bharu,
and a Singapore blog that

107
00:06:53,363 --> 00:06:57,252
tracks old film locations
has identified scenes shot in

108
00:06:57,332 --> 00:07:01,582
Singapore and Malacca, including
the old Orchard Road Police

109
00:07:01,662 --> 00:07:05,775
Station standing in for the
police station of Kota Bharu.

110
00:07:05,775 --> 00:07:10,491
On the 17th of July 1943 the
Syonan Shimbun reported that the

111
00:07:10,571 --> 00:07:15,602
Board of Information in Tokyo had
decided to send "Harimau Malai"

112
00:07:15,682 --> 00:07:20,400
to the occupied south, and it
printed a summary of the plot.

113
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:23,959
In the film's story, Tani's
"baby sister was killed by

114
00:07:24,039 --> 00:07:27,800
a Chinese Communist", and
"since the murderer was used by

115
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,169
the British Secret Service,
it was natural for the

116
00:07:31,249 --> 00:07:35,225
British authorities to refuse
to help him find the killer".

117
00:07:35,225 --> 00:07:39,150
Tani becomes "the leader of
an anti-British secret body",

118
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:43,084
raids "rich British homes"
and gives "all his riches and

119
00:07:43,164 --> 00:07:47,450
money to poor and sick people",
who come to call him "Tiger".

120
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:50,482
He tracks down and kills
his sister's murderer,

121
00:07:50,562 --> 00:07:53,726
and he dies "in a terrific
struggle" stopping the

122
00:07:53,806 --> 00:07:57,050
British from blowing up
a dam on the Perak River.

123
00:07:57,050 --> 00:08:01,274
Set beside the 1933 newspaper
report, in which the attacker

124
00:08:01,354 --> 00:08:05,141
was arrested on the spot
and no motive was disclosed,

125
00:08:05,221 --> 00:08:09,883
and beside his death from malaria
in hospital, the film's version

126
00:08:09,963 --> 00:08:13,975
shows how far the story had
been reshaped in ten years.

127
00:08:13,975 --> 00:08:17,550
The nickname has caused its own confusion.

128
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:21,591
General Yamashita Tomoyuki,
who commanded the Japanese

129
00:08:21,671 --> 00:08:25,865
invasion of Malaya, was also
called the Tiger of Malaya,

130
00:08:25,945 --> 00:08:29,150
and the name is better known today as his.

131
00:08:29,150 --> 00:08:33,475
After the war the legend
took new forms in Japan.

132
00:08:33,475 --> 00:08:38,564
Kaiketsu Harimao, a television
drama loosely modelled on Tani,

133
00:08:38,644 --> 00:08:42,316
ran on Nippon Television
in 1960 and 1961 and

134
00:08:42,396 --> 00:08:47,151
was the first Japanese drama
filmed in colour, with a hero

135
00:08:47,231 --> 00:08:51,650
who fought Western colonial
rulers in Southeast Asia.

136
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:55,860
In 1989 the director Wada
Ben made the film Harimao,

137
00:08:55,940 --> 00:09:01,550
which portrayed him as a tragic
young man used by army intelligence.

138
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:05,180
In Singapore, Teater
Ekamatra staged Tiger of

139
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:09,715
Malaya at the Drama Centre
Black Box in September 2018,

140
00:09:09,795 --> 00:09:13,175
in English, Malay, Japanese and Mandarin.

141
00:09:13,175 --> 00:09:18,032
In it, a group of actors re-enact
scenes from the 1943 film and

142
00:09:18,112 --> 00:09:23,050
try to place themselves among
its heroes, villains and victims.

143
00:09:23,050 --> 00:09:27,872
Why it matters today: Tani Yutaka
is remembered in Singapore mainly

144
00:09:27,952 --> 00:09:31,896
through a memorial stone in
the Japanese Cemetery Park,

145
00:09:31,976 --> 00:09:37,025
but his story shows how quickly a
life can be turned into propaganda.

146
00:09:37,025 --> 00:09:41,641
The few records from his own time,
a 1933 newspaper report and the

147
00:09:41,721 --> 00:09:46,692
date of his death, sit uneasily beside
the hero that wartime newspapers

148
00:09:46,772 --> 00:09:50,961
and a feature film made of him,
and many of the details told

149
00:09:51,041 --> 00:09:55,950
about him today still come from
accounts written long after the fact.

150
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:59,700
Reading the story against the
newspapers of the day is one

151
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:03,875
way to separate what can be
checked from what was added later.
